WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.904 --> 00:00:12.190
Marco Polo, who was born in Venice (now in Italy) in 1254, is one of history's best-known explorers.

2
00:00:13.091 --> 00:00:17.593
He traveled farther into Asia than any other Westerner before him.

3
00:00:18.714 --> 00:00:25.577
Marco Polo was a great storyteller and, in his tales, described many Eastern customs and inventions.

4
00:00:26.638 --> 00:00:34.723
Noodles, fireworks, and pizza—to name only a few—all originated in the East and were then adopted by Westerners,

5
00:00:34.944 --> 00:00:38.406
who developed their own variations based on Polo's descriptions.

6
00:00:39.627 --> 00:00:46.273
He also inspired many future explorers with A Description of the World, a book he wrote "so that

7
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:48.395
men might know the things that are in the world."

8
00:00:49.375 --> 00:00:55.981
Polo himself thought this work, now better known as The Travels of Marco Polo, was his greatest achievement.

9
00:00:57.402 --> 00:01:01.217
The Polos were a family of merchants, and travel was in their blood.

10
00:01:02.138 --> 00:01:09.262
In 1260, Marco's father, Niccolo, and his uncle, Maffeo, set off for Asia to do business there.

11
00:01:10.083 --> 00:01:13.625
They ended up spending the next ten years traveling throughout Asia.

12
00:01:14.546 --> 00:01:20.310
During this journey, they encountered Kublai Khan, the ruler of the vast Mongol Empire.

13
00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:28.075
The Great Khan had conquered China and expanded the Mongol nation of his grandfather, the feared and revered

14
00:01:28.395 --> 00:01:29.216
Genghis Khan.

15
00:01:30.252 --> 00:01:34.933
Kublai Khan enjoyed encountering Westerners and was very curious about their culture.

16
00:01:35.774 --> 00:01:41.815
He sent the Polos back to Italy with a letter to Pope Clement IV, asking him to send

17
00:01:42.055 --> 00:01:47.477
one hundred missionaries to teach Kublai Khan and his people about Christianity and the West.

18
00:01:48.677 --> 00:01:53.699
When Niccolo and Maffeo finally returned to Venice, they were faced with many changes.

19
00:01:54.719 --> 00:01:57.740
Niccolo's wife had died, as had Pope Clement IV.

20
00:01:59.084 --> 00:02:03.446
But Niccolo's young son, Marco, had grown up during his father's travels.

21
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:10.950
When Marco turned 17 years old in 1271, the three of them set off to return to the

22
00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:11.991
Mongol Empire.

23
00:02:13.112 --> 00:02:20.175
Niccolo and Maffeo wanted to travel by sea because land travel had many drawbacks, such as endless deserts,

24
00:02:20.536 --> 00:02:23.337
towering mountains, and bandits along the roads.

25
00:02:24.458 --> 00:02:28.360
So they sailed across the Mediterranean Sea to what is now Israel.

26
00:02:29.784 --> 00:02:35.968
Once in Israel, they rode on camels through Turkey and Iran until they reached the Persian Gulf near

27
00:02:36.008 --> 00:02:37.289
the Strait of Hormuz.

28
00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:42.633
They would have sailed to China from there, but the ships in the port did not appear seaworthy,

29
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:45.655
so they decided to travel by land instead.

30
00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:50.678
The Polos made several stops as they trekked through central Asia.

31
00:02:51.599 --> 00:02:56.662
They may have stayed in the mountains of Afghanistan for as long as a year, most likely due

32
00:02:56.702 --> 00:02:57.243
to illness.

33
00:02:58.363 --> 00:03:03.599
As they traveled, Marco absorbed the sights and sounds of the civilizations around him.

34
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:10.084
He learned several languages: Arabic, Turkish, Persian, and even some Mongolian.

35
00:03:11.005 --> 00:03:16.229
Marco also learned about Buddhism, Islam, and many other religions along the way.

36
00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:24.595
After they crossed the Pamir Mountains, the Polos found the Silk Road, an ancient caravan route, and followed

37
00:03:24.635 --> 00:03:25.596
it through Asia.

38
00:03:26.604 --> 00:03:31.906
The Silk Road has existed since the time of the Roman Empire, when it was used by traders

39
00:03:32.026 --> 00:03:33.966
traveling between Rome and China.

40
00:03:35.006 --> 00:03:40.728
Travelers would sail across the Mediterranean Sea from Rome to the Persian city of Tyre.

41
00:03:41.608 --> 00:03:45.429
From there, the route went through the Pamir Mountains of Afghanistan.

42
00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:53.172
After cutting through the Pamir Mountains, travelers had to cross the Takla Makan Desert, which would bring them

43
00:03:53.232 --> 00:03:54.692
to the Great Wall of China.

44
00:03:55.812 --> 00:04:01.375
Once they reached the Great Wall, they followed it to the city of Xi'an, one of the ancient

45
00:04:01.455 --> 00:04:02.456
capitals of China.

46
00:04:03.417 --> 00:04:08.519
The Silk Road was the route the Polos took to reach the Mongol capital, Shangdu.

47
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:16.484
After a four-year journey, the Polos were heartily welcomed at the court of Kublai Khan—especially the young Marco,

48
00:04:16.844 --> 00:04:19.326
who kept the khan spellbound with his stories.

49
00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:26.873
Kublai Khan was often bored by his visitors, but Marco's anecdotes about crossing the Gobi Desert and his

50
00:04:26.953 --> 00:04:29.434
other adventures intrigued the Great Khan.

51
00:04:30.534 --> 00:04:36.856
He was so impressed by Marco's language and story-telling abilities that he asked Marco, his father, and his

52
00:04:36.956 --> 00:04:38.737
uncle to stay and work for him.

53
00:04:40.257 --> 00:04:45.679
No one is sure what position Marco held in the Great Khan's government; some think he was governor

54
00:04:45.739 --> 00:04:49.980
of the city of Yangzhou, while others say he was only a tax collector.

55
00:04:50.956 --> 00:04:58.219
Whatever his position, he traveled extensively throughout the Mongol Empire, all over China, and as far south as

56
00:04:58.279 --> 00:05:04.802
Myanmar. Everywhere he went, Marco carefully observed the customs of the people he met.

57
00:05:05.883 --> 00:05:11.025
He was amazed by the monasteries in Tibet and by the use of paper money instead of coins

58
00:05:11.105 --> 00:05:12.406
made from precious metals.

59
00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:21.106
Marco also saw messengers carrying letters in a relay—one of the world's most efficient early postal systems.

60
00:05:22.247 --> 00:05:27.870
But after 17 years of traveling and working in the Mongol Empire, the Polos wanted to go home.

61
00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:35.154
The Great Khan was reluctant to see them go, but allowed them to return to Venice in 1292.

62
00:05:36.454 --> 00:05:41.097
Marco arrived in Venice in 1295 and started a trading business.

63
00:05:42.038 --> 00:05:48.066
But in 1298, Venice went to war with the city of Genoa, and Marco was taken prisoner.

64
00:05:49.227 --> 00:05:55.854
While in prison, he met the writer Rustichello da Pisa and began recounting all the wonders he had

65
00:05:55.914 --> 00:05:56.734
seen in the East.

66
00:05:57.936 --> 00:06:04.903
He described a multitude of inventions, as well as many scientific observations he had made—such as the fact

67
00:06:04.943 --> 00:06:10.088
that, at high altitudes, fire does not burn as well and water doesn't boil as quickly.

68
00:06:10.932 --> 00:06:17.735
He also regaled Rustichello with stories about spirits who try to lure travelers into the desert and about

69
00:06:17.835 --> 00:06:19.616
Kublai Khan's lavish banquets.

70
00:06:20.616 --> 00:06:26.778
Rustichello transcribed Marco Polo's tales of adventure and called them A Description of the World.

71
00:06:27.659 --> 00:06:33.821
The book became an immediate success—a remarkable feat before the invention of the printing press, when books were

72
00:06:33.861 --> 00:06:35.202
still copied out by hand.

73
00:06:36.102 --> 00:06:40.224
A Description of the World was the most widely read travel book of the Middle Ages.

74
00:06:41.036 --> 00:06:46.741
The exotic lands Polo described inspired many explorers to embark on their own adventures.

75
00:06:47.742 --> 00:06:52.545
Even Christopher Columbus supposedly had a copy of it with him when he set out to find a

76
00:06:52.625 --> 00:06:55.268
new sea route to India in 1492.

77
00:06:56.228 --> 00:07:02.153
But not everyone was impressed by Polo's book; many people thought that his accounts were embellished or even

78
00:07:02.233 --> 00:07:03.174
completely false.

79
00:07:04.135 --> 00:07:10.400
To this day, historians continue to search for evidence to corroborate or refute Marco Polo's claims.

80
00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:17.303
The facts are hard to pin down, however, because Polo's original book no longer exists, and its many

81
00:07:17.363 --> 00:07:19.685
translations often contradict each other.

82
00:07:21.005 --> 00:07:26.248
Regardless of whether the journeys described in A Description of the World are true or not, there is

83
00:07:26.368 --> 00:07:30.831
no denying that Marco Polo had a profound impact on the history of the world.

84
00:07:31.771 --> 00:07:38.695
America might never have been discovered by Columbus if Polo's tales of adventure hadn't inspired explorers to look

85
00:07:38.755 --> 00:07:40.116
for a new route to the East.

86
00:07:41.152 --> 00:07:47.255
Polo's travels in Asia and time spent with the Great Khan also led to the West's discovery of

87
00:07:47.335 --> 00:07:50.617
many products from the East, such as oils and spices.

88
00:07:51.757 --> 00:07:58.640
These valuable resources encouraged traders to travel between the East and the West more frequently, leading to more

89
00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:00.781
open relations between these worlds.

90
00:08:02.062 --> 00:08:08.600
And when skeptics asked Polo to renounce his "fables" on his deathbed, he reportedly said that his book

91
00:08:08.681 --> 00:08:12.188
did not contain even half of the marvels he had seen.
